            <ArticleTitle>Chronic Urticaria and Irritable Bowel Syndrome: a Cross-Sectional Study of 11,271 Patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/bjd.15997</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The human body interacts with the environment mainly through three major tissues: the lungs, skin, and gastrointestinal (GI) tract. In each one, an intensive nervous network is present, conjugated with abundant mast cells (MC). While MC-mediated disorders in airways and the skin are extensively discussed, MC-mediated disorders of the GI tract remain relatively uncommon. Since the GI tract is one of the three main environment-interacting tissues, it is reasonable that MC-mediated disorders of the GI may have the same weight and impact as asthma or chronic urticaria (CU). This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
